[17592186120020 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1045 "The Shortest Distance Between a Plane and a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120020 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e15d96c3-59f3-5ea3-8c30-d6c68ac605ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1061 17360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1731663-b6e6-5bf1-a62a-a1b6ab66b554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1061 17361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02b00fc5-d232-5394-bd52-5c011f1db096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1061 17362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "214a0b2c-29b3-58b2-a7c5-0300d4430cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1061 17364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dffaaec1-80eb-5582-bd94-3dde9c7b5f0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1061 17940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e02b271-c1ce-518a-b549-4e83a2061ea7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1061 17941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3779ed6b-358c-5101-8bd7-9de56abc2ade" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1061 17942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "213ff9e2-fe7a-528b-b535-4cf580c6aca9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120727 #edb/ref 1061 17943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fdb4920a-a24d-544c-bdef-93ed800bfdd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1065 3178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5f8c6994-f448-5679-8d2e-5591b86c2a97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121029 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the shortest distance between the point $P(3,0,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ that is given by the equation \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\langle 2 , 3 , 1 \\rangle ) \\cdot \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, note that $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 4 , 4 , 2 \\rangle$ is a vector that's normal to the plane.\n\n![](images/7b/7b2dfcd87c1f5e4e2c2b1a0b9a7f73899772e33e1020465b2b38d1b2de29a66c.png)\n\nLet's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(2,3,1)$. Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,1⟩-⟨3,0,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe shortest distance $d$ from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26e494eef47db4dba3ac36dfa96c7e9a9d191321b22bb82874f3e74bbd7116ef.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+12+6|}{\\sqrt{16+16+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is $\\dfrac{7}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1065 3261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "224c4104-9097-5a92-9c37-5d0fe22171a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121054 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124495 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175